 {"id":"fabro-5082","title":"Engine: oversized checkpoint event exceeds the events-endpoint body limit (413) and kills the run — two 2h merge-upstream runs destroyed mid-flight","status":"in_progress","type":"bug","priority":0,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T13:19:08.232Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T14:56:35.113Z","description":"WHAT HAPPENED (2026-09-11, two instances): MergeUpstream runs die irrecoverably at their conflict-resolution checkpoint with `worker lost canonical run store during append run event: request failed with status 413 Payload Too Large: Failed to buffer the request body: length limit exceeded` (event=checkpoint.completed). Instance 1: run 01M27V69K6BVS8RNEK3PAAT48Q died 09:29 after 28m (also `Git push failed` immediately before — likely the same oversized-payload class on the push path or a consequence). Instance 2: run 01M2837TP51DNK159MHVPTCVVK died 13:13:36 after 111m of healthy conflict-resolution work (tool calls seconds before death, 0 agent errors) — the accumulated checkpoint payload simply crossed the HTTP body limit. Conductor passes 01M27V3Y12QD and 01M2834T4ZWY routed 'Merge child failed' correctly; ~2.5h of merge work was destroyed. ROOT CAUSE: the worker->server event append (POST /runs/{id}/events, the canonical store) has a request body length limit with no ceiling-aware path for large checkpoint payloads; a run whose serialized state (diff summaries, context, journals of a big merge) grows past it dies at the next checkpoint with no truncation, chunking, or blob offload. KNOWN PRIOR ART: evidence capture already enforces a 16 KB inline ceiling with blob-ref detours elsewhere; the checkpoint event path lacks the equivalent. FIX DIRECTIONS (engine, fabro-server/fabro-workflow): (a) offload oversized event payloads to the blob store and inline a reference (preferred — no data loss); (b) or chunked append; (c) at minimum, fail the checkpoint with a resumable error instead of stopping the workflow run. IMPACT: every large merge (busy fork + high upstream drift) hits this — the merge leg is effectively capped by payload size, not by conflict complexity. Manual /merge-upstream remains the workaround. Basis: runs 01M27V69K6BVS8RNEK3PAAT48Q and 01M2837TP51DNK159MHVPTCVVK (+ conductors 01M27V3Y12QD, 01M2834T4ZWY), server log 13:13:36 UTC, 2026-09-11","labels":["engine","workflows"]}
 {"id":"fabro-a723","title":"checkpoint.completed event exceeds 3MB body limit: 413 kills run AFTER successful merge","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T14:01:43.433Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T14:56:35.025Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-11 on merge-upstream runs 01M27V69 and 01M2837: the worker appends checkpoint.completed whose JSON body exceeds MAX_RUN_EVENT_BODY_BYTES (3MB, fabro-types/src/run_event/mod.rs). Server rejects with 413 Payload Too Large, event sink loses the canonical store, workflow stops, run is marked failed(workflow_error) — even though the merge commit already exists and the gate passed. Result: complete work stranded in an ephemeral sandbox, conductor pass soft-stops, next pass redoes ~2h and ~2 of LLM work.\n\nAgent tool outputs are already bounded (fabro-agent truncation.rs MAX_SERIALIZED_TOOL_OUTPUT_BYTES = limit/2; tool_execution.rs event_body_budget). The workflow-side checkpoint event has NO equivalent bound: whatever it inlines (reports/journal/diff?) can exceed the limit.\n\nFix direction: bound or blob-offload large fields in checkpoint (and any other workflow-emitted) events before POST, mirroring the agent-side truncation contract; add a regression test that builds a >limit checkpoint event and proves the worker truncates instead of losing the store.","closedAt":"2026-09-11T14:56:35.025Z"}
 {"id":"fabro-6beb","title":"fabro/run/* branch pushes rejected by branch protection: finished run work is never persisted remotely","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T14:01:43.531Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T17:36:20.627Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-11: run lifecycle pushes of refs/heads/fabro/run/<id> (and possibly fabro/meta/* in some cases) are rejected by GitHub with 'github rejected the ref - likely a branch protection rule or push ruleset' (runs 01M27V69 11:29, 01M2837 15:13). Consequence: when a sandbox dies or a run fails late, the only copy of finished work is the ephemeral sandbox filesystem; salvage requires docker cp from stopped containers.\n\nFix direction: allow-list fabro/run/* and fabro/meta/* refs in the denkhaus/fabro repo ruleset (ops change), and/or make the engine fall back to a pushable ref namespace on rejection and surface the persistent failure in run notices. Without this, any post-merge infrastructure failure is unrecoverable-by-design.","labels":["needs-user"]}
